= Unit 5 · Tensor products

== Lecture 9 · Linearizing bilinear maps
#focus[Construct a tensor product, use its universal property, and distinguish pure tensors from arbitrary tensors. All modules in this unit are over a commutative ring $R$.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall a map out of a quotient must kill its defining relations.


A bilinear map accepts two module elements and is linear in each variable. The tensor product replaces this two-input mapping problem by an ordinary linear mapping problem. Its elements are formal combinations constrained by bilinearity, not pairs with componentwise operations.

#definition[Tensor product][The tensor product $M otimes_R N$ is a module with a bilinear map $(m,n)|->m otimes n$ such that every bilinear $b:M times N -> P$ factors uniquely as a linear map $overline(b):M otimes_R N -> P$ satisfying $overline(b)(m otimes n)=b(m,n)$.]

#strategy[Begin with a free module on symbols and impose exactly the bilinearity relations; maps descend precisely when those relations vanish.]

#theorem[Existence by generators and relations][Start with the free $R$-module on symbols $[m,n]$. Quotient by the submodule generated by
$ [m+m',n]-[m,n]-[m',n], quad [m,n+n']-[m,n]-[m,n'], $
$ [r m,n]-r[m,n], quad [m,r n]-r[m,n]. $
The quotient, with $m otimes n$ the class of $[m,n]$, is a tensor product.]
#proof[The relations make the symbol map bilinear. A bilinear $b$ extends uniquely from the free basis to a linear map on the free module, and the displayed relations lie in its kernel. It therefore descends uniquely to the quotient. Conversely any linear map from the quotient gives a bilinear map by composition.]

Every tensor is a finite sum of pure tensors $m otimes n$. It need not itself be pure. For vector spaces, $e_1 otimes e_1+e_2 otimes e_2$ in $K^2 otimes K^2$ is not pure: a pure tensor gives a coefficient matrix of rank at most one, while this tensor gives the identity matrix of rank two.

#theorem[Basic tensor identifications][There are canonical isomorphisms $R otimes_R M iso M$, $M otimes_R N iso N otimes_R M$, and $(M otimes_R N)otimes_R P iso M otimes_R(N otimes_R P)$.]
#proof[For the first, send $r otimes m$ to $r m$, with inverse $m |->1 otimes m$. The tensor relations verify both composites on pure tensors. For symmetry, interchange the two inputs; the operation is bilinear and is its own inverse. For associativity, both objects classify trilinear maps from $M times N times P$; equivalently define the map on pure tensors by $(m otimes n)otimes p |->m otimes(n otimes p)$, check multilinearity through the two universal properties, and reverse the parentheses for its inverse. Pure tensors generate, so the inverse equations hold everywhere.]

#example[Why a tempting tensor formula fails][Over $ZZ$, the proposal $m otimes n mapsto m+n$ from $ZZ otimes ZZ$ to $ZZ$ is not well defined by the universal property. The proposed two-input function is not bilinear: at $(0,n)$ it gives $n$, whereas a bilinear function must vanish when either input is zero. In contrast $m otimes n mapsto m n$ is induced by multiplication and identifies the tensor with $ZZ$.]

Testing zeros and scalar movement is a fast preliminary check, but a valid proof checks additivity and scalar compatibility in each argument. Only then may a formula on pure tensors be extended linearly.

#checkpoint("5.1", [Why is $r m otimes n=m otimes r n$?], [Both expressions equal $r(m otimes n)$ by the bilinearity relations. This scalar movement is part of the tensor construction.])
#takeaways[Define maps on pure tensors only after verifying the relevant bilinearity relations. A formula on symbols is not automatically a well-defined linear map.]
