== Lecture 10 · Computation and right exactness
#focus[Compute tensors with quotients, prove right exactness, and exhibit the failure of left exactness.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall that pure tensors generate, though not every tensor is pure.


#strategy[Translate a quotient presentation into tensor relations, then identify the resulting kernel as an image.]

#theorem[Tensoring with a quotient ring][For an ideal $I$, $(R slash I)otimes_R M iso M slash I M$, through $[r]otimes m |->r m+I M$.]
#proof[Changing $r$ by an element of $I$ changes $r m$ by an element of $I M$, so the formula is well defined and bilinear. Its inverse sends $m+I M$ to $[1]otimes m$. Every generator $im$ of $I M$ maps to $[i]otimes m=0$, so this inverse is well defined. The composites are identities on the indicated generators.]

#example[Tensor products of finite cyclic groups][Over $ZZ$, $(ZZ slash m ZZ)otimes(ZZ slash n ZZ) iso ZZ slash gcd(m,n)ZZ$. Apply the quotient formula to obtain $(ZZ slash n ZZ)/m(ZZ slash n ZZ)$, which is $ZZ slash(m ZZ+n ZZ)$. Bézout identifies the sum ideal with $gcd(m,n)ZZ$.]

#theorem[Right exactness][If $A arrow.r^f B arrow.r^g C ->0$ is exact, then $A otimes N -> B otimes N -> C otimes N ->0$ is exact.]
#strategy[Identify $C$ as the quotient of $B$ by the image of $f$, then show tensoring imposes exactly the corresponding generated relations.]
#proof[Write $C=B/K$ with $K="im"f$. The quotient of $B otimes N$ by the submodule generated by $k otimes n$, $k in K$, has the tensor universal property for $(B/K)otimes N$: a bilinear map on $B times N$ descends to $(B/K)times N$ exactly when it vanishes on $K times N$. Thus it is canonically $C otimes N$. Its kernel is precisely the submodule generated by the $f(a)otimes n$, which is the image of $f otimes 1$. Surjectivity follows because every pure tensor $c otimes n$ has a lift $b otimes n$.]

#example[Injectivity can be destroyed][Tensor the injection $ZZ arrow.r^(times 2) ZZ$ with $ZZ slash 2ZZ$. Under the identifications with $ZZ slash 2ZZ$, the resulting map is multiplication by two, hence zero. Its source is nonzero, so it is not injective.]

#definition[Flatness][A module $N$ is flat if tensoring with it preserves injections; together with right exactness this means it preserves short exact sequences. Free modules are flat because tensoring with a free module gives a direct sum of copies, and direct sums preserve injections coordinatewise.]

#example[Computing a tensor in two complementary ways][For $ZZ/6ZZ otimes ZZ/10ZZ$, the generator $[1]otimes[1]$ is killed by both six and ten, hence by their gcd two. The quotient formula identifies the whole tensor with $ZZ/2ZZ$, proving the generator has exactly that order rather than merely order dividing two. Equivalently, multiplication modulo two gives a bilinear map detecting it as nonzero.]

An annihilation calculation gives an upper bound on size; a detecting map often supplies the missing lower bound. This pattern is useful whenever a formal relation seems to make a tensor vanish but no explicit inverse or universal property has yet been checked.

#checkpoint("5.2", [What is $QQ otimes_ZZ ZZ slash n ZZ$ for positive $n$?], [It is $QQ slash n QQ=0$, since multiplication by $n$ is surjective on $QQ$.])
#takeaways[Use presentations to calculate tensors. Preserve surjectivity and the image/kernel relation, but never assume a tensor map remains injective without a flatness argument.]
